\title{The Silver Growth Rate of the Berggren Tree of Pythagorean Triples}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{9 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Every primitive Pythagorean triple occurs exactly once in Berggren's ternary tree, rooted at
$(3,4,5)$; in Euclid coordinates the three generating moves are the linear maps
$B_1(m,n)=(2m-n,m)$, $B_2(m,n)=(2m+n,m)$, $B_3(m,n)=(m+2n,n)$ acting on seeds $(m,n)$ with
$m>n>0$, $\gcd(m,n)=1$, $m\not\equiv n \pmod 2$. Embedding a seed in the hyperbolic upper
half-plane by $z(m,n)=(n+i)/m$ gives every triple a hyperbolic distance $d(i,z(m,n))$ from
the base point $i$, and the identity $\cosh d(i,z(m,n))=(m^2+n^2+1)/(2m)$ makes this an
exact elementary function of the seed. We determine the relationship between this metric
quantity and the combinatorial depth of a node completely. We prove the logarithmic window
$\log m\le d\le \log m+\log 2$; the existence of a silver potential
$\Phi(m,n)=m+(\sqrt2-1)n$ with $\Phi(B_iv)\le(1+\sqrt2)\Phi(v)$ for all three moves and
every seed, with equality for $B_2$; the sharp envelope $d\le(k+1)\log(1+\sqrt2)+\log2$ at
depth $k$ with a matching lower bound along the Pell spine of pure middle moves, so that the
metric growth exponent of the tree is exactly $\log(1+\sqrt2)=0.88137\ldots$; a trichotomy
for the three pure branches, with rates $0$, $\log(1+\sqrt2)$, $0$, refuting the conjecture
that the parabolic move $B_1$ is the sole obstruction to positive growth and showing that
the value $\log 3$ suggested by ternary branching is attained by no path; the corrected
two-sided law $(\#B_2(w)+1)\log2\le d\le(|w|+1)\log(1+\sqrt2)+\log2$ in terms of the
labelling word $w$; and the optimal depth law: hypotenuse $N$ first occurs at depth
$\log N/(2\log(1+\sqrt2))+O(1)$, attained by the near-isoceles Pell triples.
\end{abstract}

